INT. SUNSET SOUND STUDIO - CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT

Egg-crate foam, a mixing desk the size of a pool table. Beyond the glass, a vocal booth with a single microphone.

DAVID KESSLER, 50s, producer, sits at the desk. Next to him is DAVID KESSEL, 20s, an intern who has been mistaken for him all week. KIM, singer, 30s, stands in the booth. Her brother KIP, 30s, leans on the wall of the control room eating a sandwich nobody offered him. Over the talkback, the engineer, RHONDA, 40s, speaks from somewhere in the building.

                    RHONDA (V.O.)
Is everyone rolling or is it just me

                    DAVID KESSLER
(pressing the talkback, patient)
Kim, honey, we are on take forty-one. I'm going to say this one time with love. The song is about heartbreak. You sound like you're ordering a coffee.

                    KIM
(through the glass)
Maybe I am ordering a coffee. Maybe that's the heartbreak. Maybe heartbreak is mostly just standing in a line.

                    KIP
(mouth full)
That's actually good. You should say that on the record.

                    DAVID KESSEL
(raising a hand)
Sorry, hi, I think that's David's note to give. Mine. Not mine. His.

                    DAVID KESSLER
(not turning)
Kessel, you can't be both.

                    DAVID KESSEL
(beat)
I'm just saying I wrote the bridge.

                    KIM
(into the mic)
He wrote the bridge, Kip wrote the chorus, and I wrote the part where I leave.

                    RHONDA (V.O.)
(dry)
Great news the part where you leave is cheaper to mix

                    KIP
(putting the sandwich down, standing)
Kim. Are you leaving us or the song?

                    KIM
(a long beat)
I don't know. Roll the tape.

                    DAVID KESSLER
(quietly, to Kessel)
Press record.

                    DAVID KESSEL
(pressing it, trembling)
Rolling. Take forty-two.
